reply:
{
  "questionAndScope": "Whether primary duck hepatocytes maintained in a defined medium accumulate intracellular lipid under fatty-acid loading, as a first step toward a cultivated foie gras. In scope: isolation, attachment, 72 hours of culture, loading with oleate, lipid and viability measurement. Out of scope: scale, flavour, and regulatory approval of a food.",
  "background": [
    {
      "text": "Primary duck hepatocytes have been kept proliferating in a defined serum-free medium for 96 hours, so a culture window of 72 hours is within what has been reported.",
      "claimIds": [
        "src:file:paper-a:v1:cl1"
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "Oleate loading induces lipid accumulation in human HepG2 hepatoma cells; this is the loading regime proposed here, transferred to an avian primary cell with the species difference stated as an assumption.",
      "claimIds": [
        "src:file:paper-b:v1:cl1"
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "Stirred suspension culture did not support goose hepatocytes in the one report available, so this design uses attached culture on collagen-coated plates rather than suspension.",
      "claimIds": [
        "src:file:paper-c:v1:cl1"
      ]
    }
  ],
  "hypothesis": "Primary duck hepatocytes in a defined medium accumulate intracellular lipid within 72 hours of oleate loading, measurable as at least a two-fold rise in triglyceride content per mg protein over vehicle, with viability maintained.",
  "modelAndPrerequisites": "Primary hepatocytes from Pekin duck liver by two-step collagenase perfusion, plated on collagen-coated dishes in a defined serum-free medium. Prerequisites: fresh liver supply from the institution's source; an isolation run validated on one liver for attachment efficiency above 60 percent; HepG2 cells available as the positive control line.",
  "variablesControlsComparators": "Independent variable: oleate-BSA at 400 micromolar versus BSA vehicle. Comparators: HepG2 cells under the same loading as the positive control; unloaded duck hepatocytes at each time point. Three donor livers; three wells per condition per time point.",
  "endpointsAndMeasurementPlan": "Primary endpoint: triglyceride content per mg protein by enzymatic assay at 0, 24, 48 and 72 hours. Secondary: Oil Red O signal by elution at 510 nm; viability by trypan blue at each time point. All measurements in the units of the internal unit table; nothing converted.",
  "successFailureInconclusiveCriteria": "Success: triglyceride content at 72 hours at least two-fold that of vehicle in at least two of three donors, with viability above 80 percent. Failure: no rise above 1.2-fold in any donor, or viability below 50 percent in loaded wells with vehicle unaffected. Inconclusive: triglyceride content between 1.5-fold and 2-fold of vehicle at 72 hours with viability above 80 percent, or any result where fewer than two of three donors agree in direction; the study is then repeated with a second loading regime before any conclusion is drawn.",
  "resourcesTimelineDependencies": "Three duck livers over three weeks; collagenase perfusion set-up; a plate reader; two technicians for three weeks; the HepG2 line and oleate-BSA stock. Depends on the isolation validation run and on the internal unit table carrying the assay units.",
  "limitations": "Primary cells dedifferentiate within days, so the window is short; donor variability is expected; oleate alone may not reproduce the overfeeding that produces foie gras; the lipid class accumulated may differ from the product's fat.",
  "alternatives": "Lipid accumulation in a chicken hepatocyte cell line as a surrogate model was not chosen: it does not address the biological source facet, which is mandatory, and a result in a chicken line would not answer the question in the target species. A suspension-culture variant was not proposed because the one available report found suspension did not support goose hepatocytes.",
  "outcomesAndDecisionPoints": "If success: proceed to a duration and scale study in the same model. If failure: test a second loading regime (palmitate-oleate mix) once before concluding that fatty-acid loading alone is insufficient in duck hepatocytes. If inconclusive: repeat with a second loading regime and a fourth donor before any decision. A named reviewer decides at each point."
}
